WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:08.640
PolyPhen-2 is an automatic tool&nbsp;
for prediction of the possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:13.200
impact of an amino acid substitution on the&nbsp;
structure and function of a human protein.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:18.080
Automated predictions of this kind are&nbsp;
essential for interpreting large datasets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:22.720
of rare genetic variants, which have many&nbsp;
applications in modern human genetics research.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:28.800
Uses in recent research include identifying&nbsp;
rare alleles that cause Mendelian disease,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:33.200
scanning for potentially medically&nbsp;
actionable alleles in an individual’s genome,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:38.400
and profiling the spectrum of rare variation&nbsp;
uncovered by deep sequencing of large populations.&nbsp;

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:45.360
PolyPhen predicts functional effects of amino acid&nbsp;
variations based on both multi-sequence alignment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:52.480
and protein 3D structure features. It is based on&nbsp;
three presumptions. The first is the same as in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:57.520
SIFT, that amino acid variations at conserved&nbsp;
positions are more likely to cause functional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:03.360
changes. The second presumption is that amino&nbsp;
acid variations that affect the active sites,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:09.360
interaction sites, solubility, or stability of a&nbsp;
protein are likely to affect protein structure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:14.240
The third presumption is that changes&nbsp;
in protein structure are likely to cause&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:18.320
changes in protein function, which are&nbsp;
likely to cause changes in phenotype.&nbsp;

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:24.080
PolyPhen-2 uses eight sequence-based and three&nbsp;
structure-based predictive features which were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:29.840
selected automatically by an iterative greedy&nbsp;
algorithm. Majority of these features involve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:34.000
comparison of a property of the wild-type&nbsp;
allele and the corresponding property of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.600
the mutant allele, which together&nbsp;
define an amino acid replacement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:43.280
Most informative features characterize how well&nbsp;
the two human alleles fit into the pattern of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:47.920
amino acid replacements within the multiple&nbsp;
sequence alignment of homologous proteins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:52.320
how distant the protein harboring the first&nbsp;
deviation from the human wild-type allele is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:58.000
from the human protein, and whether the mutant&nbsp;
allele originated at a hypermutable site. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:03.040
alignment pipeline selects the set of homologous&nbsp;
sequences for the analysis using a clustering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:08.880
algorithm and then constructs and refines their&nbsp;
multiple alignment. The functional significance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:13.840
of an allele replacement is predicted from its&nbsp;
individual features by Naïve Bayes classifier&nbsp;

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:22.320
In this example, we will investigate the&nbsp;
effects of p53 R273H mutation. In the “Protein&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:30.160
or SNP identifier” text box, enter a protein&nbsp;
identifier. PolyPhen-2 uses the UniProtKB database&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:36.400
as a reference source for all protein sequences&nbsp;
and annotations. You can also enter human protein&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:40.720
identifiers from other databases such&nbsp;
as RefSeq or standard gene symbols.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:47.040
However, UniProtKB identifiers are preferred&nbsp;
as the most reliable and unambiguous.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:53.680
Leave the “Protein sequence in FASTA format”&nbsp;
text box empty. Enter the position of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.680 --> 00:03:00.480
substitution in the protein sequence into the&nbsp;
Position text box. For this example, type 273.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:06.240
Select the appropriate boxes for&nbsp;
the wild-type amino acid residue AA1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:13.360
and the substitution residue AA2. For this&nbsp;
example, select “R” for AA1 and “H” for AA2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:18.480
respectively. Enter an optional description&nbsp;
into the “Query description” text box.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:24.800
Click the Submit Query button. You will be&nbsp;
transferred to the Grid Gateway Interface Web&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:31.920
page. Use the Refresh button to reload the page&nbsp;
until your query job is listed under Completed.&nbsp;

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:36.640
Click the corresponding View link to browse the&nbsp;
PolyPhen-2 prediction report for your query.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:41.360
You will be presented with the Web page&nbsp;
listing your Query and the Prediction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:47.360
and Confidence panel. Prediction outcome can&nbsp;
be one of probably damaging, possibly damaging,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:54.160
or benign. “Score” is the probability of the&nbsp;
substitution being damaging. “Sensitivity” and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.160 --> 00:04:00.800
“specificity” correspond to prediction confidence.&nbsp;
The predicted damaging effect is also indicated by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:06.160
a vertical black marker inside a color gradient&nbsp;
bar, where green is benign and red is damaging.&nbsp;

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:11.120
By default, only the prediction&nbsp;
based on the HumDiv model is shown.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:17.200
Click on the [+] control box next to the HumVar&nbsp;
label to display the HumVar-based prediction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:23.200
and scores. Click on the [+] control box next&nbsp;
to “Multiple sequence alignment” to display&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:28.720
the multiple sequence alignment panel. This&nbsp;
panel displays a multiple sequence alignment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:33.600
for 75 amino acid residues surrounding your&nbsp;
variant’s position in the query sequence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:38.400
Click the link at the bottom of the panel&nbsp;
to open an interactive alignment viewer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:44.080
to scroll through the complete alignment.&nbsp;
Click on the [+] control box next to 3-D&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:54.320
Visualization to display the interactive&nbsp;
3-D protein structure viewer applet.

